# What Vercel must not look at.
#
# `api/` is the NestJS backend, and it is here because Vercel's zero-config
# treats a root-level `api/` as Serverless Functions: it found the 19 files
# in it and tried to build each one as its own handler. They are Nest
# modules and services — no default export, no request signature — so the
# build finished, "Deploying outputs..." was printed, and the deployment
# failed there. Every deployment to this project had been failing that way,
# with a build log that ends in success.
#
# The app itself never imports it. The frontend *test* suite does — the
# directory fold in `api/indexer/lobbies` is exercised from
# `tests/unit/lobbyDirectory.test.ts` — so the deploy typechecks
# `tsconfig.build.json`, which is the app without its tests. Tests are still
# typechecked, by `npm run typecheck`. The backend is deployed from this
# same repository by Render, from its own `npm run api:build`.
/api

# Leading slash on every line, and that is load-bearing. This file uses
# gitignore matching, so a bare `contracts/` matches a directory of that
# name at ANY depth — including `frontend/src/contracts/`, which holds
# `addresses.ts` and the generated deployment manifest. Writing it unanchored
# deleted the app's own contract config and failed the build with eleven
# `Cannot find module '../contracts/addresses'`.
#
# Server-only, and never part of the SPA. `contracts/out` is absent on
# Vercel anyway — `prebuild.mjs` says so and falls back to the committed
# `frontend/src/contracts/generated/`, which is the intended path there.
/contracts
/dist-server
